plan Persistence
title   Persistence: explicit durability, atomic scope writes and honest restore
status  proposed
goal
    Keep explicitly selected actor and shared-data truth in an Effect KeyValueStore under one app,
    scope and version. Restore a consistent, context-closed cut without replaying executable work.
    Corrupt or incompatible scopes start fresh with diagnostics; failed writes retain the last good
    cut and never undo a committed turn.

    This Plan replaces FlowState O8 and moves FlowState D41 here. Persistence is greenfield: neither
    package implements storage I/O. Stage S6 reuses the bounded decoder limits, fail-closed version
    validation, in-flight restore rules and newer-wins merge described in
    references/split-work/code-persistence-ssr.md, and the rewrite's restore-requires-persist rule.
    Core persistence follows S4; its entity gate also requires S5. References are examples, not proof
    that these outcomes are implemented.

    Decisions 6, 10, 17, 18, 22 and 38 in references/split-work/decisions.md bind this Plan. Decision
    38 supersedes decision 32 and the multi-tab part of 33: there is no MultiTab capability plan.
    references/design/multi-tab.md is deferred design only. Adopted rationale is in
    references/design/minimal-library.md section 10, references/design/entity-domain.md section 5.5,
    references/design/lazy-features.md section 8 and references/design/server-rendering.md section
    2.8; the binding rulings supersede their older scope statements.
outcomes
    O1  Configuration and storage
        runtimeSetup({ app, services, persistence?: { scope, version } }) selects persistence.
        Without persistence there is no persistence acquisition, restore, capture or storage I/O.
        scope and version are required nonempty authored strings under the Runtime identity rules;
        version is compared exactly, not ordered. Keys are namespaced by an unambiguous encoding of
        (app id, scope), so neither another app nor another principal shares records accidentally.
        A different principal requires a new Runtime; the host supplies the scope and Flow cannot
        authenticate it (@plan.Runtime.O7).

        KeyValueStore comes from effect/persistence in Effect 4.0.5. A persistence setup requires
        that service in its Layer requirements: missing storage is a type error, never a silent
        no-op. Untyped missing provision fails ready with StorageFailure before installing actors.
        Configuration is closed: unknown or deleted fields fail construction with FlowError and
        remediation. construct() is synchronous and inert: no storage acquisition, I/O, actors or
        external work. ready() acquires the storage dependency before detached validation, then
        acquires application services; shared dependencies are acquired once, not as two Runtimes.
        The executable Layer acquisition proof is required by @V2 and @plan.Runtime.O2.

        Flow ships layerIndexedDb({ database? }) as a KeyValueStore Layer; its public route is
        coordinated with @plan.Package.O1. Effect supplies memory, file-system, SQL and Web Storage
        Layers but no IndexedDB Layer. KeyValueStore.layerStorage(() => localStorage) remains the
        small synchronous option; IndexedDB is the documented choice for larger entity stores.
        The IndexedDB Layer preserves string bytes, uses atomic per-key transactions, owns its
        database connection through the Layer scope, aborts outstanding owned transactions on
        cancellation and closes on release. Read, write, remove, open, quota and cleanup failures
        have typed storage evidence, normalized through @O5. It never clears unrelated app data.
        Selection does not depend on the storage implementation.

        Stories, including focused machine Stories, replace setup storage with a fresh in-memory
        KeyValueStore per run. Fixtures may seed that store; they inherit no browser/session data
        and never use the production setup's store (@plan.Testing.O3).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/persistence.ts (IndexedDB, user scope, version).
    O2  What is persisted
        Every independent persist flag defaults to false. actorRef(machine, id, { persist: true })
        selects a stable shared actor. createActor and local React actors are never durable.
        resource({ persist: true }) independently selects confirmed entries of that descriptor;
        a resource never selects an actor and an actor never implicitly selects resource data.
        Transactions have no persist option: their attempt and pending-outcome facts follow their
        owning actor. Streams keep opt-in persist for their latest value, and require a persisted
        owning actor for that value to be durable. Terminal and ownership facts needed to prevent
        activity replay follow the actor even when a stream value is not selected.

        Memory uses the definition's Schema; persisted resources and stream values require result
        Schemas. Every Schema used for persisted values, issue data, pending mapped events or
        Attempt input must encode/decode synchronously and without service requirements. Selecting
        an effectful or service-requiring Schema is a type error. Encoding rejects unsupported
        values with CodecFailure, never lossy coercion, dropped fields or a stringify fallback.
        Codecs perform no I/O, run no Effects and create no actors or executable boot payload.

        Actor records retain exact machine/ref identity, state path, memory, fixed context bindings
        and referenced refs; serializable issue facts; operation/activity identity and delivery facts;
        observed store/provider revisions; generation fences, stale-completion facts and ordinals;
        finite/page cursors; armed schedule deadline, generation, owner-state and consumed facts;
        stream latest/hasValue/count when selected and terminal status; pending commit Attempts and
        their invocation evidence; and pending mapped outcome payloads not yet delivered as turns.
        Revisions remain capture evidence, not a second authority for restored selected context.
        Persisted issues contain bounded diagnostic data only, never raw Cause or Error objects.

        Resource data is one canonical confirmed base, not one copy per actor. Shared optimistic
        layers and entity predictions are excluded and are not reapplied on restore. Preserve
        uncertainty provenance and the entity keys predicted by pending writes so repair can mark
        them uncertain. Entity persistence includes each persisted answer's references and context,
        and only the confirmed fields those answers project (@plan.EntityDomains.O2). Never persist
        a prediction as confirmed data or use it as a memory baseline.

        Never persist ordinary mailboxes, commands, the pre-attach buffer, fibers, scopes, callbacks,
        services, transports, sessions, buffered stream emissions, input or executable work. Admitted
        but unprocessed public events are lost on reload. Pending mapped outcomes are private
        durable facts, not a persisted general mailbox. Snapshot context selections are recomputed
        from exact providers, never serialized as context overrides. Stable wire refs use
        length-prefixed UTF-8 identity segments, never Buffer or UTF-16 length counts.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Session/session.ref.ts,
        references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/editor/editor.ref.ts and
        references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/ticket.operations.ts.
    O3  Capture and writes
        Runtime observes committed publications independently of inspector sinks. Capture runs
        between completed publications, reads the canonical store once and atomically reads each
        actor's published state. No actor's observed store revision is newer than that cut.
        Unrelated state-only turns need not have happened at the same instant. There is no public
        barrier API. Capture performs no external work, starts no lookup, emits no context event
        and mutates no Runtime truth.

        Membership is registered non-disposed durable actors and their exact transitive provider
        closure, independently selected resources and confirmed entity data. Local actors,
        tombstones, disposed actors and expired retain records are absent from the next capture;
        disposing a durable editor therefore removes its draft from the next committed capture.
        Dormant lazy records are carried forward without decoding, rewriting or loading their code
        (@plan.LazyFeatures.O3).

        The durable context graph must be closed and its propagation complete. Preserve exact
        provider refs and observed revisions; reject mixed cuts, newer revisions, closure changes,
        orphan/partial payloads, duplicate canonical bases and selected-context overrides. Context
        debt gives retryable ConcurrentCapture: Runtime retries at the next settled cut, never
        writes stale/partial data and never requires the caller to retry. A local, missing or
        non-durable required provider fails the whole selected consumer closure with terminal
        NonDurableContextProvider; no promotion, substitution, rebinding or silent omission. Other
        closures cannot be used to publish a scope that silently drops the failed selection: keep
        the previous complete manifest. Report every failing context.<slot> path, including all
        direct/transitive failures, consumer id, provider diagnostic id and provider machine.
        Remediation names actorRef(providerMachine, id, { persist: true }) and ensureActor, never
        ID-bearing createActor. Static detection belongs to @plan.CommandLine.O5.

        Schema values and envelopes use the shared canonical structural codec (@plan.Inspection.O2):
        depth 32, 10,000 nodes, 4,096 items per array, 262,144 UTF-8 bytes per string and 2,097,152
        canonical bytes per encoded record or manifest. The same limits apply to storage, SSR and
        artifacts. Scope chunking cannot bypass individual value bounds. Canonical bytes are host
        neutral and equal on Node, Bun, Deno and browsers. Codec failure writes no manifest and
        commits no Runtime mutation.

        Each scope is an atomic logical capture implemented with immutable generation records and
        a manifest written last. Each record identifies its owner kind/id and chunk; the manifest
        carries format, app id, scope, version, generation, cut revision and the complete record set
        with lengths and content digests. Use disjoint keys for changed records; unchanged dormant
        records may retain their keys. Keep two manifest slots and all records needed by the current
        and previous complete generations. Write all new records first, await acknowledgment, then
        atomically replace the next manifest slot. Only an acknowledged manifest commits the cut.
        Remove superseded records only after that commit and only if neither retained manifest
        names them. Never call clear or touch another app/scope's keys. A store must provide atomic
        set per key; Flow does not assume multi-key transactions.

        Restore chooses the highest complete compatible manifest. A torn newest set is ignored and
        the last good manifest wins; never combine generations. Generation counters are safe
        integers with headroom and never wrap. Both manifests and their record lists must fit the
        shared codec bounds; excess is CodecFailure, not silent eviction. A record set cannot grow
        an unbounded manifest.

        Writes are serialized per scope with at most one ordinary write in flight and one newest
        waiting cut. Coalesce ordinary captures at the end of the current publishing task; replace
        an older waiting cut with the latest settled cut. There is no timer debounce. An older write
        cannot overtake a newer committed generation. Attempt durability barriers (@O6) are ordered
        forced writes and cannot be coalesced away or invoked ahead of their acknowledgment.

        Flush on pagehide and visibility becoming hidden, and on Runtime disposal. Browser lifecycle
        flush is best effort: asynchronous storage cannot promise completion after the document
        dies. dispose stops admission and observation, normalizes outstanding work, then waits for
        finalizers, evidence and the last persistence write before releasing storage and the lock.
        Disposal is bounded under @plan.Runtime.O6; a never-settling commit becomes unknown. A
        storage operation exceeding the disposal bound fails with StorageFailure, preserving the
        last good manifest, rather than hanging or claiming a successful flush.
        Reference: none yet (stage S10 adds it).
    O4  Restore
        ready() requests the scope lock (@O7), acquires storage if persistence is enabled, reads
        the retained manifests/records and validates detached data, then builds application
        services. Validate envelope format, app id, scope, exact version, canonical bounds, record
        lengths/digests, receiving-app compatibility, machine/state/event/operation identities,
        schedule ownership and timing, exact refs/bindings and all counter headroom. Decode value
        Schemas without actors or store installation. Then install prepared actors and one
        canonical store, providers before consumers; complete initial ensures and resolve refs;
        seal the graph and establish silent context baselines before activation or ready handles.
        Baseline selection manufactures no onContext event. Bootstrap failure or interruption
        exposes no live actor/ready handle or external actor work, and rolls acquisition back in
        reverse order. Inert render handles remain governed by @plan.React.O1.

        A restored stable actor is Runtime-owned, active and captureable without another ensure.
        Its retain clock starts at ready; ensureActor returns it and ignores fresh input because
        restored state counts as the first accepted input. createActor stays fresh and local.
        Conflicting persist/retain/context on a ref rejects under @plan.Runtime.O3. Restore never
        runs initial, replays input or old turns, or resumes finite lookup/commit execution.

        Rebuild projections from exact refs and canonical store truth. Install pending mapped
        outcomes before continuing-activity reconciliation; deliver them once in their saved
        order as ordinary internal turns after readiness, ahead of newly admitted external events.
        Restored pending remote commits produce unknown carrying the same Attempt unless durable
        evidence excludes invocation even after the saved cut (@O6). Only proven uninvoked work
        becomes interrupt. A queued/not-yet-invoked fact alone is insufficient. Deliver unknown as
        an ordinary outcome event; authors give it an actionable uncertainty state and explicit
        retry(attempt), never leave a save permanently waiting or automatically replay it.

        In-flight resource requests do not resume. Restored data starts stale at restore; live
        bindings may initiate fresh descriptor-policy lookups after activation. Unsubscribed
        key-only entries stay passive until a live binding supplies parameters. Required staleTime
        and gcTime belong to @plan.Operations.O2; gcTime for restored unobserved entries starts at
        ready, not an invented retention default. Preserve pinned parameters for a generation;
        automatic refetch uses the oldest eligible live binding, explicit refetch its caller's
        parameters. Restore never reconstructs parameters or capabilities from a key.

        Restore entity records at position 0 and lower restored answer frontiers so live data wins.
        Mark keys of uncertain writes before their records become readable, including when the
        owner actor is dormant; repair follows @plan.EntityDomains.O2. Server hydration uses the
        merge of @plan.ServerRendering.O5: persisted actors beat hydrated actors; hydrated data
        beats restored data. Neither older restored data nor predictions overwrite newer truth.

        Duration schedules keep absolute deadlines; overdue fires once, never a burst. Multi-step
        Schedules restart at step one. Preserve owner/generation and consumed facts, and take silent
        restartOn baselines. Ancestor schedules survive descendant changes and self transitions
        (@plan.Machines.O5). A hidden tab's resume uses the same deadline rule.

        Running streams retain selected latest/hasValue/count projection and open a fresh owned
        generation after pending outcomes drain; no old emission replays. Terminal streams restore
        terminal and do not restart merely because of reload; a new activity identity may start a
        new session. Actor/global counters and generation fences continue from stored values;
        exhausted or unsafe integers reject detached validation before any installation.

        Read the whole scope, but hold records of declared unadopted lazy owners as bounded opaque
        bytes. Capture carries them unchanged. Adoption decodes the newly known closure detached
        and uses this restore ordering (@plan.LazyFeatures.O3); malformed dormant data discards the
        affected adoption closure with a diagnostic, without half installation. A version change
        discards dormant records with the scope. Capture and restore never load lazy code. Unknown
        operations/entities may be dormant where the lazy ownership envelope permits it; an orphan
        actor machine outside the declared app is incompatible, not automatically registered.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/persistence.ts and
        references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/editor/editor.machine.ts (unknown and retry).
    O5  Failure policy
        Diagnostics use the single FlowError vocabulary (@plan.Diagnostics.O2), with operation,
        owner/path, scalar details and actionable remediation. Required distinct codes are
        StorageFailure (read/write/remove/acquisition/cleanup), CodecFailure (capture Schema encode
        or bounds), IdentityVersionMismatch (format/app/scope/version or incompatible identities),
        MalformedData (structural/value decode, invalid counter or corrupt bytes), ConcurrentCapture
        (retryable context/cut debt), NonDurableContextProvider (terminal selected closure) and
        SecondTabNoPersistence (@O7). Structural, bound, application-value, I/O and cleanup causes
        remain distinguishable; no raw parser exception or undefined corruption result escapes.
        Keep full Cause through boundary cleanup, but never serialize it into storage or evidence
        carriers. No compression or decompression surface is added.

        Version mismatch, incompatible identity or corrupt scope data discards all of that scope's
        records logically, reports its diagnostic and starts fresh; ready succeeds. Torn newest
        generations first fall back to a complete older one. If none validates, discard rather
        than partially restore. Compatible shape changes use pure Schema decode transformations;
        v1 has no migration hook, fingerprint API or identity aliases. Read failure reports
        StorageFailure and disables persistence for that Runtime so unread storage is not overwritten.
        Missing required service is a setup failure (@O1), distinct from a supplied store read error.

        Write failure preserves committed Runtime truth and the last good manifest; it never fails
        an already committed actor turn. Report StorageFailure to Runtime diagnostic observers and
        inspector sinks/traces (@plan.Inspection.O3 and @plan.Inspection.O4), even with no persisted
        actor currently mounted. A later publication retries the newest cut. Codec failure skips
        the whole write with CodecFailure and no Runtime mutation. Inspection-sink failure cannot
        interrupt persistence. Cancellation and cleanup retain honest typed evidence; failed
        disposal rejects and quarantines ownership under @plan.Runtime.O6.

        runtime.dispose({ forget: true }) removes this scope's manifests before its records after
        work is fenced, awaits deletion and cleanup, and releases its lock last. A failed deletion
        reports StorageFailure and never claims the data was forgotten. A Runtime refused a lock
        performs no deletion. Principal switch always creates a new Runtime; forget is explicit,
        not an automatic consequence of an ordinary disposal.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/host.ts (sign-out and forget).
    O6  Attempt durability
        For an actor currently using persistence, mint an Attempt with its id and encoded input,
        and require an acknowledged KeyValueStore commit of that Attempt before invoking a remote
        commit adapter. This forced scope write records may-have-invoked evidence and the same
        consistent cut; invocation is forbidden until its manifest is acknowledged. Adapter
        dispatch may happen immediately after acknowledgment. A crash between acknowledgment and
        dispatch still restores unknown conservatively. Failure to acknowledge prevents invocation,
        reports StorageFailure and settles that live occurrence through interrupt; no remote side
        effect starts without the barrier. A superseded ordinary cut cannot erase this evidence.

        A last good record can predate later dispatch. Saved queued status is not proof that the
        adapter stayed uninvoked afterward: restore uses unknown unless evidence excludes that
        interval. Persisting the attempt is not an adapter journal and Flow does not recover an
        adapter-private transport or journal. Terminal outcomes already durably recorded need no
        new invocation; stale completions remain fenced by actor incarnation and attempt identity.

        unknown carries the exported Attempt Schema (id and encoded input). Explicit retry(attempt)
        reissues exactly that id and input through @plan.Operations.O3; a known settled attempt
        settles immediately without adapter invocation. Flow never creates a new id for
        reconciliation and never retries automatically. Attempt identity is not reused by a later
        Runtime; deterministic Stories use their seeded identity source.

        Non-persisted actors have no attempt durability and lose their workflows on crash. The same
        limitation applies when @O7 explicitly runs a Runtime without persistence: report that mode
        and make no durability claim. Such actors can execute independently; there is no cross-tab
        exactly-once execution guarantee. For an enabled persistence Runtime, a transient write
        failure cannot silently downgrade a selected durable commit past its barrier.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/ticket.operations.ts (idempotency key)
        and references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/editor/editor.machine.ts (retryUncertain).
    O7  Detect and refuse a second persistence owner
        At ready(), a Runtime configured with persistence requests a Web Lock with ifAvailable,
        named by the unambiguous (app id, scope) identity; version is not part of the lock name.
        Acquire it before any storage restore/capture. Hold the granted lock until disposal has
        finished final persistence/forget work and cleanup; failed bootstrap releases it. Concurrent
        same-scope readiness therefore grants at most one persistence owner in a supporting host.

        If another tab or Runtime holds it, do not wait or retry for leadership: this Runtime runs
        independently without persistence, with no restore, capture, writes or forget I/O, and
        reports SecondTabNoPersistence through Runtime diagnostics and inspection evidence.
        It remains in that mode for its lifetime even if the owner closes. A new Runtime may try
        at its own ready(). Other app/scope identities do not block one another.

        Where navigator.locks is absent, including servers and insecure origins, skip the check
        and proceed with persistence. Do not add a BroadcastChannel fallback or imply exclusive
        ownership there; per-scope atomic writes still keep a committed record set whole. No leader
        election, follower mirrors, forwarded sends, peer deltas, tab-shared actors, automatic
        handover or tab scheduler is in v1. Failure of an available lock request reports
        StorageFailure and runs without persistence rather than bypassing a failed ownership check.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/persistence.ts (decision 38 example).
decisions
    D1  KeyValueStore with scope and version
        Was FlowState D41. Effect owns storage; definition/result Schemas own encoding. Replace the
        archived storage/codec/filter object and constructors, not their error and purity guarantees.
        Decision 18 requires Flow's IndexedDB Layer and effect/persistence imports.
    D2  Explicit selection retains data, never replay authority
        Restore archived Persistence D1 with independent actor/resource selection, stream latest
        opt-in and transactions following their actor (decision 15). No automatic durability.
    D3  Atomic per-scope manifests and fresh-start compatibility policy
        Decision 17: records first, manifest last; torn sets fall back to the last good manifest.
        Corruption/version mismatch discards the scope with a diagnostic; compatible changes use
        Schema transforms. Coalesce writes, flush on page hide and disposal; no migration API.
    D4  Storage precedes detached validation
        Restore archived Persistence D2 with the acquisition correction from review-findings and
        sol-plan F1: acquire storage before reading, validate before app-service activation and
        actor installation. Bootstrap is all-or-nothing (@plan.Runtime.O2).
    D5  Durability precedes invocation
        Decision 6: persisted actor Attempts are acknowledged before remote adapter invocation.
        Reconciliation reuses the Attempt. No durability for non-persisted workflows; decision 36
        supplies the Attempt shape and explicit retry form. Old queued bytes alone never prove
        post-cut non-invocation.
    D6  Schedules retain deadlines and disposal waits for the final write
        Decision 10 keeps Duration deadlines with one overdue firing and restarts multi-step
        Schedules. Decision 22 bounds disposal, normalizes unsettled commits to unknown and waits
        for finalizers, evidence and persistence; failure retains honest cleanup evidence.
    D7  Decision 38 replaces the multi-tab design
        Same-scope Web Lock detect-and-refuse is the entire v1 ownership feature. Decision 32 and
        decision 33's multi-tab adoption are superseded, including their sharing and degradation
        protocols. No MultiTab plan is required or referenced.
constraints
    C1  Closed replacement surface
        No archived PersistenceStorage, PersistenceCodec, filter, Slot/Entry or second error family;
        no generic migration/fingerprint API, actor aliases or automatic durability. Traces,
        checkpoints and artifacts are never storage restore inputs. Server transfer has only the
        sanctioned dehydrate/Hydrate path (@plan.ServerRendering.O1 and @plan.ServerRendering.O4),
        never a generic mutable Runtime hydration or boot decoder API.
    C2  One store, exact identity, bounded non-executable bytes
        No actor-private canonical cache, serialized selected-context authority, implicit finite
        retries, terminal stream restart on reload or key-to-parameter inversion. All persisted
        bytes obey @plan.Inspection.O2 and all restored actors require stable durable refs.
    C3  No sharing protocol
        Cross-tab sync and conflict resolution are out. @O7 is detection/refusal only; absent Web
        Locks are not replaced by leader election or another synchronization mechanism.
verification
    V1  Configuration, purity and defaults
        Planned type fixtures reject missing KeyValueStore, unknown options, transaction persist,
        missing result Schemas and effectful/service-requiring persisted Schemas. Runtime fixtures
        prove default-off selection, independent resource/actor flags, zero construction I/O,
        unsupported-value CodecFailure without coercion, and fresh Story storage even when the
        setup store throws on use. Establish @O1 and @O2; no application implementation is claimed.
    V2  Bootstrap, exact identity and rollback
        Planned integration tests inject acquisition/read/app-service failure, interruption and
        disposal races at every readiness boundary. Log storage acquisition before read/decode,
        decode before application activation, one shared acquisition and reverse cleanup. Compatible
        records restore exact actors/providers without initial/input replay; app/version/state/ref/
        schedule/wiring incompatibility or malformed memory discards the scope with its diagnostic.
        No failed bootstrap exposes live handles or starts actor work. Restored actors are captured
        without ensure, restored input wins over later input, retain starts at ready and conflicting
        ref options reject. Counter exhaustion installs nothing. Establish @O4 and @O5.
    V3  Capture consistency and durable closure
        Planned race tests interleave store/actor publications, context waves, registration and
        disposal. Assert one store cut, no newer actor observations, no partial closure, duplicate
        bases or context overrides. ConcurrentCapture retries after settled propagation. A local or
        non-durable direct/transitive provider fails the whole closure; two invalid slots report
        both paths and identities with actorRef/ensure remediation. Capture calls no adapter,
        emits no context event and changes no Runtime truth. Establish @O3.
    V4  Restore without replay
        Planned tests cover passive key-only entries, fresh lookups only after live binding,
        deterministic parameter choice, stale restored data and gcTime from ready; confirmed bases
        without optimism; pending mapped outcomes before continuing activities; running streams
        with zero/latest values and fresh sessions, terminal streams without restart; overdue
        Duration once, multi-step restart, hidden-tab resume and ancestor schedule survival.
        Assert no old input, initial, lookup, commit or emission replay and monotone safe counters.
        Establish @O2 and @O4 with @plan.Operations.O2 and @plan.Machines.O5.
    V5  Delayed-write crash and attempt acknowledgment
        Planned ordering tests record storage acknowledgments and adapter calls: durable invocation
        must follow the Attempt manifest acknowledgment; a failed barrier calls no adapter, while
        non-persisted actors do not wait for storage. Capture a queued pending occurrence; allow
        its acknowledged pre-invocation record and remote dispatch; suppress newer writes; crash;
        restore the earlier saved bytes. Require unknown with the original Attempt and no new
        request. A saved queued label alone must not yield interrupt. Retry reuses the same id and
        input; a known settled Attempt never invokes. Establish @O6 and @O4.
    V6  Atomic writes, failures and disposal
        Planned fault-injecting memory, Web Storage and IndexedDB tests fail read/write/remove/open,
        quota, cancellation and cleanup. Tear every record/manifest boundary: restore one last-good
        set, never a generation mix. Delay older writes, coalesce bursts, test bounds/Schema failures
        and verify no committed actor turn fails afterward. Flush hidden/pagehide and final disposal;
        bounded hanging commits become unknown and stalled writes fail honestly. Forget removes
        manifests first, touches no unrelated key and reports failed deletion. Establish @O3/@O5.
    V7  Browser ownership and IndexedDB
        Planned Chromium, Gecko and WebKit integration gates prove simultaneous same-scope ready
        has one persistence owner, second owner reports SecondTabNoPersistence with zero storage I/O,
        different scopes/apps stay independent, owner disposal releases the lock after final flush,
        and a refused Runtime never gains persistence automatically. With navigator.locks absent,
        persistence proceeds. Lock-request failure disables persistence with evidence. IndexedDB
        reload, cancellation, connection release and quota errors follow @O1/@O5. Establish @O7.
    V8  Shared bytes, entity and lazy/SSR integration
        Planned Node/Bun/Deno/browser vectors prove identical canonical bytes and exact codec bounds.
        S6's entity gate after S5 proves projected confirmed fields only, position-0/lowered-frontier
        restore and uncertainty keys before reads. Dormant records survive repeated captures without
        loading code; version change discards them; malformed adoption is all-or-nothing. Exercise
        persisted-actor/hydrated-data precedence with @plan.ServerRendering.O5 and dormant adoption
        with @plan.LazyFeatures.O3. Establish @O2 through @O4; packed consumers run at the stage gate.
    V9  Dispatch Desk closeout
        Planned S10 Next.js App Router browser gates reload session/editor drafts, resource entries,
        confirmed entity records and pending-save uncertainty without replay. Demonstrate second-tab
        refusal and no-lock persistence in Chromium, Gecko and WebKit. The reference is currently a
        design example; no source scan or focused typecheck substitutes for these behavioral gates.
questions
    Q1  What concrete timeout bounds a stalled persistence operation during disposal?
        Decision 22 requires bounded disposal but does not name the timeout. Recommended: share the
        Runtime disposal deadline rather than add a persistence-specific public option; measure in
        S6 and settle with the Runtime owner before the disposal gate. A timeout must reject with
        StorageFailure and preserve the last good manifest, never claim a completed flush.
    Q2  What default database name should layerIndexedDb use?
        The shipped Layer and optional database option are required; a default name is not settled.
        Recommended: "flow-state", with per-app/scope key namespacing, and verify application data
        isolation with the Package owner before publishing that default.
